  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14039567/assets/56265e4a47a878233f27b1e265158dc766aa410144dfe95a20ce7f672b797802.png)\n\nThe function $f(x)$ is defined on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$\nThe graph of the second derivative $y={f}^{\u2033}(x)$ of the function $f(x)$ intersects the $x$-axis at the points $x=-4$ and $x=1,$ as shown above. Find the interval on which the function $f(x)$ is concave up.\n\n$f(x)$ is concave up on the interval\n$(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$",
  "worked_solution": "A function $f(x)$ is concave up over an interval $(a,b)$ if ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0$ for all $x\\in (a,b).$\n\nFrom the plotted graph of $y={f}^{\u2033}(x),$ we can see that ${f}^{\u2033}(x)>0$ on the interval $(-4,1).$\n\nTherefore, the function $f(x)$ is concave up on the interval $(\\,-4,1\\,).$",
